[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering State Hoisting, Side Effects, and LaunchedEffect in Jetpack Compose"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Jetpack Compose relies heavily on unidirectional data flow (UDF). Understanding how to manage state, handle side effects safely, and prevent stale closures using rememberUpdatedState is crucial for building robust Android applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. What is State Hoisting?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"State hoisting is a design pattern in Compose where a stateful composable is made stateless by moving its state to a caller. This makes composables more reusable, testable, and easier to maintain."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Stateful Composable: Manages its own internal state using remember. Harder to reuse and test."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Stateless Composable: Receives state as a value and exposes events as lambdas (callbacks). Highly reusable."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"// Stateful Composable
@Composable
py UserInputScreen() {
    var text by remember { mutableStateOf("") }
    StatelessUserInput(text = text, onTextChanged = { text = it })
}

// Stateless Composable (Hoisted)
@Composable
fun StatelessUserInput(text: String, onTextChanged: (String) -> Unit) {
    OutlinedTextField(
        value = text,
        onValueChange = onTextChanged,
        label = { Text("Enter Name") }
    )
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Understanding Side Effects in Compose"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"A side effect is any application state change that happens outside the scope of a composable function's body. Because composables can execute frequently and in any order, running side effects directly inside a composable function leads to bugs, unpredictable renders, and memory leaks."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"3. LaunchedEffect: Running Suspend Functions Safely"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"To call suspend functions safely from inside a composable, use LaunchedEffect. It enters the composition, launches a coroutine with its block, and cancels the coroutine when it leaves the composition."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"@Composable
fun TimerScreen(onTimeout: () -> Unit) {
    LaunchedEffect(key1 = true) {
        delay(3000L)
        onTimeout()
    }
    Text("Loading, please wait...")
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"4. rememberUpdatedState: Preventing Stale Closures"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"When launching long-running effects (like LaunchedEffect), the lambda inside might capture a value that changes over time. If you do not want to restart the effect when that value changes, but you still need the latest value inside the effect, use rememberUpdatedState."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"@Composable
fun LandingScreen(onTimeout: () -> Unit) {
    // Ensure we always reference the latest onTimeout lambda without restarting the effect
    val currentOnTimeout by rememberUpdatedState(onTimeout)

    LaunchedEffect(Unit) {
        delay(3000L)
        currentOnTimeout()
    }

    Box(modifier = Modifier.fillMaxSize(), contentAlignment = Alignment.Center) {
        CircularProgressIndicator()
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By correctly hoisting state, restricting side effects to controlled environments like LaunchedEffect, and utilizing rememberUpdatedState for callbacks, you ensure your Jetpack Compose UIs remain predictable, efficient, and bug-free."}]
